Whole-slide histopathology experiments are vulnerable to failures that occur before model fitting, including annotation ambiguity, contaminated patches, patient-level leakage, color variability, incompatible intermediate artifacts, and validation choices that leak into the test set. This work presents a reproducibility-oriented workflow for cancer segmentation in digital pathology and evaluates it on DIAGSET, a public prostate histopathology dataset. The workflow converts slide and annotation pairs into HDF5-backed patch datasets, performs manual-review-guided graph cleaning, generates patient-disjoint training, validation, and test splits, applies stain normalization at runtime from training-derived state, optionally reduces only the training split by embedding-based smart sampling, trains a fixed registry of segmentation models, optimizes a two-stream ensemble on validation patients, and evaluates the frozen recipe on held-out test patients. In the DIAGSET experiment, 419 valid image--annotation cases yielded 1,186,778 retained patches across 393 patients; the held-out test set contained 79 patients and 387,325 patches. No stain-normalization strategy was uniformly best across evaluation criteria. The non-normalized setting obtained the highest pixel-level micro Dice (0.8415; 95\% CI 0.7767--0.8987) and patient-level macro Dice (0.7540; 95\% CI 0.6710--0.8356), Ruifrok obtained the highest AUROC point estimate (0.9579) and positive-patient Dice (0.7985; 95\% CI 0.7189--0.8688), and Macenko obtained the highest DIAGSET-style patch balanced accuracy before patient suppression (AvAcc 0.9009). However, all settings still produced false positives in at least 11 of 41 negative test patients. The results support the workflow as an auditable experimental protocol, while showing that clinical interpretation remains limited by false-positive control and by evaluation on a single dataset.
